.TH KENFS 5 "August 2026" "kenfs 0.9.0" "File Formats and Mounting"
.SH NAME
kenfsmount, kenfs \- mount a Research Unix filesystem image; filesystem format (V4 through 32V)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kenfsmount
[\fB\-6\fR|\fB\-7\fR|\fB\-32\fR] [\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-f\fR] [\fB\-d\fR]
.I image mountpoint
.br
.B kenfsmount
[\fB\-6\fR|\fB\-7\fR|\fB\-32\fR]
.B \-c
.I image
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kenfsmount
mounts a Research Unix filesystem image (as used by a PDP-11) as a FUSE
filesystem, so files can be copied on and off the image for use with a
simulator such as SIMH
.BR pdp11 (1).
.PP
One binary serves every edition we care about.  The on-disk format is the
1969 Thompson/Canaday/Ritchie design \(em a flat i-list at a fixed offset,
directories as ordinary files of fixed-size entries, and device files \(em
carried essentially unchanged from the PDP-7 through V6, then widened once,
in V7.  V4, V5 and V6 are byte-for-byte identical on disk; 32V is V7
recompiled for the VAX, with one byte-order caveat noted under
.B "FILESYSTEM FORMAT"
below.
.PP
The first form mounts the image at
.IR mountpoint .
The second form
.RB ( \-c )
does not mount; it walks the free list and the inode table and reports any
integrity problems on standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-6
Select the V6 on-disk format.  This also covers V4 and V5, which are identical
on disk.
.TP
.B \-7
Select the V7 on-disk format (the default).
.TP
.B \-32
Select the 32V (VAX) on-disk format: the same layout as V7, but every 32-bit
field and the 3-byte block addresses are little-endian rather than PDP-11
middle-endian (see
.B "FILESYSTEM FORMAT"
below).
.TP
.B \-r
Mount read-only.
.TP
.B \-f
Stay in the foreground (do not daemonize).
.TP
.B \-d
Enable FUSE debug output (implies
.BR \-f ).
.TP
.B \-c
Run the integrity check instead of mounting.  The image is opened read-only
and the free list and inode table are walked; a non-zero exit status means
problems were found.
.SH LOCKING
For the whole duration of a mount,
.B kenfsmount
holds an exclusive
.BR flock (2)
on the image file.  This is the hook a patched simulator uses to pause its own
disk I/O while the image is mounted, so the two writers never touch the image
at once.  Do not run the simulator while the image is mounted: the running
kernel caches the superblock free list, the inode table, and the buffer cache,
and editing the image behind it leaves those stale.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
mkdir mnt
kenfsmount -7 rp06-0.disk mnt         # V7 image
kenfsmount -6 v6root.dsk mnt2         # V6 (also V4/V5) image
cp host.txt mnt/tmp/             # copy a file onto the image
cp mnt/etc/passwd .              # copy a file off
fusermount3 -u mnt               # unmount

kenfsmount -6 -c v6root.dsk           # free-list / inode-table check
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; non-zero on error, or (for
.BR \-c )
when integrity problems were found.
.SH FILESYSTEM FORMAT
The rest of this page folds together the historical
.IR fs (5)
(V4, V6) and
.IR filsys (5)/dir (5)
(V7, 32V) pages into a single description of the whole V4-through-32V range.
.SS Layout common to all editions
Every volume is divided into 512-byte blocks.
.PP
.nf
    block 0         unused (bootstrap program, pack label, ...)
    block 1         super block
    blocks 2..      i-list (one or two blocks per inode, see below)
.fi
.PP
A directory is an ordinary file (its type bit is set in the mode) whose data
is a sequence of entries.  From V4 onward each entry is 16 bytes:
.PP
.nf
    struct direct { unsigned short d_ino; char d_name[14]; };
.fi
.PP
The first two entries are, by convention,
.B .
and
.BR .. .
.PP
(V3 and earlier used a 10-byte entry \(em a 2-byte i-number and an 8-character
name \(em so a reader for those editions must use a different directory
walker.  The 16-byte entry dates from V4, and is the same struct that
survives verbatim into V7's
.IR dir.h .)
.SS The super block (block 1)
.I V4, V5, V6 \(em all 16-bit fields
.PP
.nf
    struct {
        int   isize;        /* +0:  first block after the i-list */
        int   fsize;        /* +2:  first block not allocatable */
        int   nfree;        /* +4:  free-block count (0..100) */
        int   free[100];    /* +6:  free block numbers */
        int   ninode;       /* +206: free i-number count (0..100) */
        int   inode[100];   /* +208: free i-numbers */
        char  flock;        /* +408: lock flag (in-core only) */
        char  ilock;        /* +409: lock flag (in-core only) */
        char  fmod;         /* +410: modified flag (in-core only) */
        char  ronly;        /* +411: (V6) read-only flag */
        int   time[2];      /* +412: last superblock update (32-bit) */
    };
.fi
.PP
.I V6 only:
the V6
.I filsys.h
appends
.B "int pad[50];"
after
.IR s_time[2] ,
making the struct 516 bytes \(em 4 bytes past the 512-byte block.  It does
not spill into block 2: V6's
.I bcopy
counts in 16-bit words, and the superblock I/O copies 256 words (512 bytes),
so the last 4 bytes of the pad are never written.  V4 and V5 have no pad.
.I V7, 32V \(em widened block addresses
.PP
.nf
    struct filsys {
        unsigned short s_isize;      /* blocks in i-list + superblock */
        daddr_t        s_fsize;      /* first block not allocatable */
        short          s_nfree;      /* free-block count */
        daddr_t        s_free[NICFREE];  /* free blocks (NICFREE=50) */
        short          s_ninode;     /* free i-number count */
        ino_t          s_inode[NICINOD]; /* free i-numbers (100) */
        char           s_flock, s_ilock, s_fmod, s_ronly;
        time_t         s_time;       /* last update */
        daddr_t        s_tfree;      /* total free blocks (unmaintained) */
        ino_t          s_tinode;     /* total free inodes (unmaintained) */
        short          s_m, s_n;     /* interleave factors */
        char           s_fname[6], s_fpack[6];
    };
.fi
.PP
The free list is a stack plus dump blocks.  The superblock caches up to
NICFREE (V6: 100, V7: 50) free block numbers.  When the cache empties, the
just-popped block is read as a dump block whose first word is a count and
whose remaining words are the next batch of free block numbers (V6: 100
16-bit entries; V7: 50 24-bit entries).  A zero block number marks the end
of the chain.
.PP
.I s_ninode
is handled the same way: the superblock caches up to NICINOD (100) free
i-numbers, and when it empties the i-list is scanned to refill it.  This
list only speeds allocation; the authoritative free/allocated state is the
inode's mode field.
.SS The inode
.I V4, V5, V6 \(em 32 bytes, 16 per block
.PP
.nf
    struct {
        int   flags;     /* +0:  mode + type bits */
        char  nlink;     /* +2:  link count */
        char  uid;       /* +3:  owner */
        char  gid;       /* +4:  group */
        char  size0;     /* +5:  HIGH byte of the 24-bit size */
        int   size1;     /* +6:  LOW 16 bits of the 24-bit size */
        int   addr[8];   /* +8:  block numbers (or device number) */
        int   atime[2];  /* +24: last access */
        int   mtime[2];  /* +28: last modification */
    };
.fi
.PP
The size is 24 bits, stored as
.BR "(size0 << 16) | size1" .
I-number
.I i
lives in block
.BR "(i+31)/16" ,
at byte offset
.BR "32*((i+31) mod 16)" .
Mode bits:
.PP
.nf
    0100000  allocated
    0060000  file type: 000000 plain, 0040000 dir, 0020000 char, 0060000 block
    0010000  ILARG (large file)
    004000/002000/001000  setuid/setgid/sticky
    000400/000200/000100  owner r/w/x (and 000070 group, 000007 other)
.fi
.PP
.I V7, 32V \(em 64 bytes, 8 per block (INOPB=8)
.PP
.nf
    struct dinode {
        unsigned short di_mode;
        short          di_nlink, di_uid, di_gid;
        off_t          di_size;       /* 32-bit */
        char           di_addr[40];   /* 13 block addresses x 3 bytes */
        time_t         di_atime, di_mtime, di_ctime;
    };
.fi
.PP
.I di_mode
is encoded like
.IR stat (2) 's
.I st_mode .
The 40 address bytes hold 13 addresses of 3 bytes each, packed by
.IR l3tol (3)
specifically to be independent of host word order.
.SS File storage (block mapping)
.I V6 \(em small and large files
.PP
A file without the ILARG bit is
.I small :
its eight
.I addr
entries are direct block numbers, so it is at most 8 x 512 = 4096 bytes.
.PP
A file with ILARG set is
.I large :
.I addr[0..6]
are seven single-indirect blocks (256 16-bit pointers each) and
.I addr[7]
is one double-indirect block.  The extra-large form is not marked by a mode
bit, only by
.I addr[7]
being non-zero.  The scheme would allow 256 x 256 x 512 = 33,554,432 bytes,
but the 24-bit size field caps a file at 16,777,216 bytes; 16-bit block
numbers cap a volume at 32 MB.
.PP
.I V7
.PP
The 13 addresses are ten direct blocks, then single, double and triple
indirect blocks, each holding 128 pointers of type
.IR daddr_t .
.I di_size
is a full 32 bits, so the triple-indirect limit is reachable.
.SS Byte order
The PDP-11 is middle-endian: 16-bit fields are little-endian; 32-bit fields
.RB ( daddr_t ,
.IR off_t ,
.IR time_t )
are stored high word first, each word little-endian.  The 3-byte block
pointers in
.I di_addr
are the low three bytes of that layout, i.e. [hi, lo, mid].
.PP
.I 32V caveat:
32V is V7 recompiled for the VAX, and
.I every
multi-byte field of 32 bits or more flips byte order.  The 3-byte
.I di_addr
addresses are packed
.B [lo, mid, hi]
instead of V7's
.BR [hi, lo, mid] ,
and
.IR di_size ,
the three timestamps,
.IR s_fsize ,
.IR s_free[] ,
.IR s_time ,
and the indirect-block
.I daddr_t
entries are little-endian instead of middle-endian.  The two kernels'
.I iexpand
functions differ in exactly where the zero pad byte lands \(em byte 1 on the
PDP-11, byte 3 on the VAX.  Only the 16-bit fields are byte-order neutral.
Treat a 32V pack as little-endian throughout;
.B kenfsmount
selects it with
.BR \-32 .
.SS Reserved i-numbers
.I V4, V5, V6:
i-number 1 is the root directory; no bad-block file exists.
.I V7, 32V:
i-number 1 is the bad-block file \(em a regular file with
.I nlink = 0
(so it has no name) whose
.I di_addr
entries are the bad block numbers themselves, marking them allocated so the
allocator never hands them out.  Root moves to i-number 2.  The stock V7
.I mkfs
creates an empty bad-block file; the list is supplied by a site's own disk
analysis.
